WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.629
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:09.480
Neural style transfer just appeared four years
ago, in 2015.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.860
Style transfer is an interesting problem in
machine learning research where we have two

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:20.280
input images, one for content, and one for
style, and the output is our content image

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.660
reimagined with this new style.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:28.221
The cool part is that the content can be a
photo straight from our camera, and the style

00:00:28.221 --> 00:00:32.650
can be a painting, which leads to the super
fun results that you see here.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.969
However, most of these works are about photos.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.219
So what about video?

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:43.749
Well, hold on to your papers because this
new work does this for video and the results

00:00:43.749 --> 00:00:45.379
are marvelous.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:50.699
The process goes as follows: we take a few
keyframes from the video, and the algorithm

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:57.270
propagates our style to the remaining frames
of the video, and, WOW, these are some silky

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.440
smooth results.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.289
In specific, what I would like you to take
a look look at is the temporal coherence of

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:04.640
the results.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:10.750
Proper temporal coherence means that the individual
images within this video are not made independently

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.750
from each other, which would introduce a disturbing
flickering effect.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.590
I see none of that here, which makes me very,
very happy!

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:25.579
And now, hold on to your papers again because
this technique does not use any kind of AI.

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:29.289
No neural networks and other learning algorithms
were used here.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.330
Okay, great, no AI.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:36.020
But, is it any better than its AI-based competitors?

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.170
Well, look at this!

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.289
Hell yeah!

00:01:42.289 --> 00:01:47.579
This method does this magic through building
a set of guide images, for instance, a mask

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:53.689
guide highlights the stylized objects, and
sure enough, we also have a temporal guide

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:59.020
that penalizes the algorithm for making too
much of a change from one frame to the next

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.420
one, ensuring that the results will be smooth.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.829
Make sure to have a look at the paper for
a more exhaustive description of these guides.

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:12.410
Now if we make a carefully crafted mixture
from these guide images and plug them in to

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:17.290
a previous algorithm by the name StyLit, we
talked about this algorithm before in the

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:22.720
series, the link is in the video description,
then, we get these results that made me fall

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.200
out of my chair.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.690
I hope you will be more prepared and held
on to your papers.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.300
Let me know in the comments!

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.770
And you know what is even better?

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:37.010
You can try this yourself because the authors
made a standalone tool available free of charge,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:42.600
just go to ebsynth.com, or just click the
link in the video description.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.160
Let the experiments begin!

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.120
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

